nodecode-jira is a rewrite of LikelyLucid's LikelyLucid/hermes-jira-browser:

  https://github.com/LikelyLucid/hermes-jira-browser
  commit 03ba860d7241af620860968fa747b109d4664cc2
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Hermes Jira Browser contributors (LICENSE)

The plugin was a Hermes Desktop board over a Python service: a Board/List
route, saved JQL views, a ticket drawer, project and worktree mapping, session
links, pull-request and CI context, and four read-only agent tools over all of
it. This rewrite keeps the four tools -- jira_assigned_issues,
jira_search_issues, jira_issue_detail and jira_issue_transitions -- and the
rules that made them safe: JQL and page sizes bounded, the issue key
validated before it becomes a URL, every answer labelled untrusted reference
data, paging through next_page_token, and credentials that go only to the
configured https origin over Basic auth. They speak Jira Cloud's REST API (v3)
directly through nlk:http, with the Atlassian Document Format flattened to
text so a model can read descriptions and comments. Two settings that the
Desktop UI existed to compute are kept as data: the story points custom field
(auto-detected, or named) and the untrusted warning itself.

What was left out: the Desktop surface, which a Nodecode cell has no part in
(board, list, drawer, saved views, keyboard shortcuts, toasts); the worktree,
project-mapping, session-link and pull-request/CI context, which are Hermes's
own projects and sessions and have no meaning here; the comment and transition choice
writes, with their idempotency receipts, because the original exposed them to
the model as read-only anyway; attachment thumbnail fetching (names and sizes
are answered, not bytes); comment pagination beyond the first 100; and the
owner-only 0600 credential file, since a cell takes its credential from the
section, the environment, a token file the operator names, or the Jira CLI's
config, all read-only. Redirects are followed by the shared HTTP layer rather
than refused as the original did, so `base_url` is checked to be the site's
own https origin with no path. Nothing of the original is vendored: there was
no code to reuse, only the wire it spoke and the rules it enforced.
